All three functions are implemented and `python3 test_impl.py` prints `ALL PASS` (stress loop took 3.2s); `test_impl.py` is untouched.

- **`encode`** in `rle.py`: groups consecutive characters and emits each character followed by its run length.
- **`decode`** in `rle.py`: reads each character plus the digits after it and expands the run.
- **`merge`** in `intervals.py`: sorts by start and folds each interval into the previous one when it overlaps or touches. It returns new lists rather than modifying the input.

I ran the suite after each function as asked: the first two runs failed on the next unimplemented function, as expected, and the third passed.

One limitation is inherent to the format: strings that contain digits don't round-trip (`encode("11")` gives `"12"`, which decodes as `"1"` twice only by coincidence, and mixed cases break). The docstrings and tests don't cover this, so I left it.
